@node CMacs Deixis Ask
@chapter CMacs Deixis Ask: ``explain THAT''

Deictic questions.  CMacs Deixis (@pxref{CMacs Deixis}) binds a spoken
``this'' or ``that'' to what the pointer rested on while the word was
said, and runs deictic @emph{commands} with it.
@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-deixis-ask.el} adds @emph{questions}: point at
something and ask about it --- ``what is that?'', ``explain this'',
``summarise that'', ``what does this error mean'', ``translate that into
German'', ``why is this failing'', ``compare this and that'', ``and that
one?'' --- and what was pointed at becomes the model's context.

There are three stages.  @emph{Extractors}, most specific first, turn
the referent into text (a public registry,
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-register-extractor}).  A privacy gate runs before
anything is read, and what was read from outside Emacs's buffers is
redacted.  Then @code{cmacs-ai-textops-stream} streams the answer into
the @code{cmacs-ai-output} window, which keeps its session for
follow-ups (@kbd{C-c C-c}) and promote-to-chat (@kbd{C-c C-p}).

There is no configure flag and no mode of its own.  By voice it rides
@code{cmacs-deixis-mode}; by hand it is @kbd{M-x
cmacs-deixis-ask-at-pointer} and an entry in the AI right-click menu.
The provider and model are the text operations' unless
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-provider} and @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-model} say
otherwise.

@menu
* Deixis Ask Doors::          By voice, and by hand.
* Deixis Ask Extractors::     From referent to content.
* Deixis Ask Questions::      What is, and is not, taken as a question.
* Deixis Ask Privacy::        What is refused and what is redacted.
* Deixis Ask Customization::  The variables.
* Deixis Ask Internals::      Additions to Deixis, reaches into other files, tests.
@end menu

@node Deixis Ask Doors
@section Two doors

@subheading By voice

@code{cmacs-deixis-filter} resolves an utterance's demonstratives, tries
the deictic commands, and then runs
@code{cmacs-deixis-unclaimed-functions} with @code{(TEXT BINDINGS)}
until one returns non-@code{nil}.  @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-utterance} is
on that hook (an autoload cookie puts it there).  Because the hook is
asked only @emph{after} the commands, a question can never take an
utterance a command matches.

When the utterance is a question, each distinct thing pointed at is
checked for privacy (one refusal refuses the whole question, aloud); the
loop is put in @code{thinking} with its reply marked open, which is what
makes talking over the answer a barge-in; the things are read one after
the other by callbacks; and the answer streams into the result window
@emph{and} into the loop's own sentence queue
(@code{cmacs-voice-loop--feed-reply}, @code{cmacs-voice-loop-speak}) ---
the path a chat reply takes, so @code{cmacs-voice-loop-speak} @code{nil}
silences it, and the loop returns to listening, with the follow-up
window open, when the speech is done.  The result window is not selected
for a spoken question.

@emph{Barge-in.}  The loop enters @code{hearing} from @code{thinking} or
@code{speaking} only when you talk over a reply.  It stops the speech
itself; this file's state hook then drops an extraction still in flight
and cancels the model's stream, when
@code{cmacs-voice-loop-barge-in-cancels-stream} is on.  Text that still
arrives is shown but not spoken.

@emph{Follow-ups.}  ``And that one?'', ``what about this?'' continue the
conversation with a new referent: the new thing is read and sent as a
follow-up turn on the @emph{same session}, through the window's own
compose region --- rendered, sealed, recorded for promote-to-chat and
streamed exactly as a typed @kbd{C-c C-c} follow-up, with what was sent
there to read.  A draft you had typed in the compose region is put back.
A follow-up needs a settled answer no older than
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-followup-seconds} (300); otherwise --- the window
closed, the answer cancelled by a barge-in, too long ago --- the earlier
question is asked afresh about the new thing.  A follow-up form with no
earlier question at all is not a question.

@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-voice} @code{nil} turns the voice door off.

@subheading By hand

@kbd{M-x cmacs-deixis-ask-at-pointer} asks about what the pointer is on
@emph{now}.  What ``that'' is comes from
@code{cmacs-deixis-referent-now}: the active region, unless the pointer
is on another window; else what the pointer is on; else point.  The
compositor's pointer ring is the one place that knows which window is
under the pointer, so when it is not sampling it is switched on for the
one lookup and off again, which empties it.

The question is read after the privacy gate has passed.  Over another
program's window, or while another program has the keyboard, it comes
from a gowl menu card with the rows of @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-menu} and
a last row for typing something else in a floating minibuffer (the AI
field commands' chooser, reused).  With the keyboard in Emacs it is one
minibuffer read with those questions as completions.  A question that
names the whole thing (``what is this window'') reads the whole thing.

Bind it in the compositor so that it works over any window:

@example
(gowl-add-keybind "Super+Ctrl+q" 'custom "(cmacs-deixis-ask-at-pointer)"
                  "Ask about what the pointer is on")
@end example

@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-register-menu} adds ``Ask about this...'' to the
AI menu's Ask group through @code{cmacs-ai-register-action}, so it is in
the right-click menu, the @kbd{C-c a} transient and
@code{cmacs-ai-run-action}.  It asks about the click's target, not the
pointer: by the time a menu row is chosen the pointer is on the menu.

@node Deixis Ask Extractors
@section From referent to content

@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-extract} asks the gate, then tries the extractors
that apply to the referent, in order, until one answers.  It never
blocks and gives up after @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-extract-timeout}
seconds.

@table @code
@item gsurf @r{(order 10)}
A buffer showing a gsurf web view: URL, title, and the selection or the
text nearest the pointed place.
@item field @r{(order 20)}
Another program's window that has the keyboard: the text
@emph{selected} in its focused field.
@item buffer @r{(order 50)}
A position, region or point in an Emacs buffer: the thing there, its
diagnostics, its file and major mode.
@item ocr @r{(order 60)}
Any other window, with a compositor: tesseract over a box around the
pointed place, or over the whole window.
@end table

@subheading An Emacs buffer

@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-buffer-thing} chooses what is ``at'' the
position, the most specific of, in the buffer's own terms:

@table @code
@item region
A region referent, or the active region when the position is inside it.
@item shown
The question was about the whole file or buffer: what the window shows
of it.
@item compilation-error
A compilation message is there: its line and the lines under it that
carry no message of their own (gcc's excerpt and caret), up to
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-error-lines}; the file and line it is about come
from the data @code{compilation-next-error} works from.
@item subtree
Org: the subtree.
@item defun
Code: the function, by tree-sitter when the buffer has a parser, else
the major mode's.
@item paragraph
The paragraph.
@item lines
The line is blank or nearly so: it and
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-context-lines} either side.
@item line
The line.
@end table

Anything longer than @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-max-chars} (6000) is cut to
that much around the pointed position, on whole lines, and the prompt
says so.  Narrowing is respected.  The flymake diagnostics over the
thing's span are added --- eglot reports through flymake --- which is
what lets ``why is this failing'' on a function see what the checker
says about it.  The description names the major mode and the file.

@subheading A web view

A gsurf buffer has no text in it, so it has its own extractor, ahead of
@code{buffer}.  One asynchronous script
(@code{cmacs-gsurf-run-javascript-async}) returns JSON: the title, the
selection, the text nearest the pointed place, and the start of the
page's text.  ``Nearest'' is the element under the pointed place,
widened to an ancestor until it holds
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-web-min-chars} (200) characters.  The place is
the frame pixel the referent carries (@code{:frame-x}, @code{:frame-y})
as a fraction of the window's body; the middle of the view when there is
none.  Only numbers are formatted into the script.  A selection beats
the pointed place; ``this page'' takes the page.

@subheading Another program's window

@emph{(a) The field.}  Only when the pointed window holds the keyboard
--- text-input reaches the focused window and no other --- and only for
a @emph{selection}: a headless edit-field session
(@code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-read-async}) reads the field, the selection
is taken, and the session is released at once.  The deny list and the
password refusal are that file's own.  The clipboard fallback is never
used: it types Ctrl+A and Ctrl+C at the application.  A field with a
caret and no selection is not this extractor's.

@emph{(b) The screen.}  @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-ocr-region} is a box of
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-ocr-box} (900x420 layout pixels) centred on the
pointed position and moved to lie inside the window, so it never reaches
into a neighbour; a smaller window, and ``what is this window'', read
the whole window.  The capture and the asynchronous tesseract run are
the gowl tools' @code{cmacs-gowl-tools--screen-text} --- what the
@code{gowl_screen_text} tool calls --- entered as a model would enter
it, so its guard refuses a region that shows a window the rules
withhold.

@emph{(c) The picture.}  When @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-images} is on
(the default) and the model takes images
(@code{cmacs-ai-vision-supported-p} for @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-provider}
/ @code{-model}, else the text operations'), the same OCR box is also
captured as a PNG, in memory (@code{cmacs-ai-capture-region}), and sent
@emph{with} whatever OCR read: OCR helps with small text, the picture
with everything else.  OCR may then find nothing and the question is
still answerable --- the picture is its material.  The capture is
refused, and the question with it, while a window the rules exclude is
visible in the box.  The window's title and app id stay home as before.
``Compare this and that'' over two windows sends two crops, numbered in
the prompt (``attached image 1'', ``attached image 2'') in spoken
order; ``and that one?'' sends its crop with the follow-up.  When the
model cannot take images nothing changes: OCR text only, and no text
read is still unanswerable.  @xref{CMacs AI Images}.

@subheading Adding an extractor

@example
(cmacs-deixis-ask-register-extractor
 'pdf
 :order 30
 :applies (lambda (referent _scope)
            (with-current-buffer (plist-get referent :buffer)
              (derived-mode-p 'pdf-view-mode)))
 :extract (lambda (referent _scope done)
            ;; Call DONE exactly once, now or later.
            (funcall done (list :source 'pdf
                                :description "a page of a PDF"
                                :text (my-pdf-page-text referent)))))
@end example

@code{:extract} calls @var{done} with a context plist (@code{:source},
@code{:description}, @code{:text}, optional @code{:extra},
@code{:foreign}, @code{:clipped}), with @code{nil} (``not mine after
all: try the next''), with @code{(:failed WHY)} or with
@code{(:refused WHY)}.  It must not block.  Signalling counts as
@code{nil}, with the error kept for the final report.  Mark text that
did not come from an Emacs buffer @code{:foreign} so that it is
redacted.

@subheading Two referents

``Compare this and that'' binds two demonstratives; each distinct thing
is read by its own extractor and the prompt carries two numbered
contexts, with the words marked:

@example
The user pointed at something on screen and said:

  compare this [this=①] and that [that=②]

Compare the two: what they share, then how they differ.

① text from an Emacs buffer (c-mode; file ~/src/a.c; lines 10-42; ...)
<<<
...
>>>

② text read by OCR off the part of a window around the pointed
position of another program; expect recognition errors ...
<<<
...
>>>
@end example

Unlike @code{cmacs-deixis-annotate} there is no legend: the legend names
windows by title, and the blocks say what each thing is.  One thing said
twice is one context.

@node Deixis Ask Questions
@section What is taken as a question

@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-match} decides.  An utterance is a question only
when @emph{every demonstrative resolved to something that still exists},
and then when either holds:

@enumerate
@item
Its canonical form (@code{cmacs-deixis-canonical}, with
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-lead-ins} such as ``so'' and ``okay'' taken off
the front) matches one of @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-questions}.
@item
The catch-all (@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-catch-all}, on by default): its
first word is one of @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-question-words} @emph{and}
the voice route it was on is one of
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-catch-all-routes} (@code{chat} and agent routes:
the ones whose utterances were going to a model anyway).
@end enumerate

The shipped questions:

@table @code
@item what-is
``what is that'', ``what's this'', ``what is this window''.
@item explain
``explain this'', ``could you describe that to me''.
@item summarise
``summarise that'', ``sum that up'', ``summarize this page''.
@item translate
``translate that'', ``translate this into German''.
@item meaning
``what does this error mean'', ``what does that say''.
@item why
``why is this failing'', ``why doesn't that compile''.
@item compare
``compare this and that'', ``what is the difference between this and
that'', ``how does this differ from that''.
@item and-that
``and that one?'', ``what about this?'' --- a follow-up.
@end table

What is deliberately @emph{not} taken: anything a deictic command
matches (commands are tried first); anything with no demonstrative, or
one that points at nothing; requests to @emph{do} something (``can you
close that'', ``make this bigger'' --- ``can'', ``could'', ``would'' and
``will'' are not in the question words on purpose: a model with tools on
the chat route can carry the request out, and an answer in a window
cannot); on the @code{commands} route or a function route, anything but
the shipped questions; and a follow-up form when there is no question to
follow up.  Everything not taken goes on to its route exactly as before.

@code{cmacs-deixis-define-question} is the sibling of
@code{cmacs-deixis-define-command}.  The shapes differ --- a question's
``body'' is always the same, so there is no argument list and no body:

@example
(cmacs-deixis-define-question who-wrote
    "\\`who wrote <object>\\'"
  "Who wrote the pointed thing."
  :instruction "Name the author if the text says; do not guess.")
@end example

@code{:instruction} is added to the prompt under what was said.
@code{:followup t} makes the utterance continue the last answer.

@node Deixis Ask Privacy
@section Privacy

Decided before any text is read, and again on what was read.  All of it
runs under @code{cmacs-redact-as-model}: a question the user asked is
still text composed for a model.

@itemize
@item
The window pointed at is put to @code{cmacs-redact-window} (app id,
title, pid); the buffer to @code{cmacs-redact-buffer-p}; a web view to
the buffer check and @code{cmacs-redact-url}.  Excluded means refused,
and nothing is read --- a page's script never runs.
@item
A window on @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field-deny} is refused whole: the
field read by the edit-field code, the OCR read by
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-deny-applies-to-ocr} (default on).  A password or
PIN field is refused by the edit-field code whatever the lists say.
@item
Text from a field, OCR or a web page goes through
@code{cmacs-redact-text}; if that fails the text is dropped, not sent.
The text of an Emacs buffer is sent as it is.
@item
A window's title and app id are not sent unless
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-send-window-identity}, and then as the rules
rewrite them.  A web page's URL and title are sent, the URL as
@code{cmacs-redact-url} rewrites it, the title through
@code{cmacs-redact-text}.
@item
A picture of the OCR box is refused while a window the rules exclude is
visible in it, or while it covers one of this Emacs's frames that shows
an excluded buffer; the question is then refused whole.  The picture is
never redacted --- an image cannot be rewritten the way text can --- so
the answer is all or nothing.  It is held in memory only.
@item
When the rules cannot be asked, the answer is a refusal (fail closed),
in the gate and in the text.
@end itemize

A refusal is @emph{visible} (the echo area, and a toast on the gowl bar;
@code{cmacs-deixis-ask-toast}) and @emph{spoken} when the question was.
Every privacy refusal reads @code{cmacs-deixis-ask-refusal-text}, the
same words whatever was matched.  A refused spoken question is still
@emph{taken}: it does not go on to another model by the chat route.

@emph{Why OCR and field text are redacted when the AI field's is not.}
The AI field commands send a field verbatim, because the user asked for
that exact text to be rewritten.  Here the text is whatever happened to
be in a box around a pointer, and a box catches things nobody pointed
at.  So it is redacted.  The text of an Emacs buffer is not: the buffer
check has passed, the thing is exactly what was pointed at, and the
other @code{cmacs-ai} actions send buffer text the same way.

@emph{Why the deny list stops OCR too.}  The list names windows whose
text fields are never read.  Reading the same window's pixels is the
same breach by another road.

@emph{Nothing blocks.}  Under @command{emacs --gowl} all of this runs on
the compositor's thread.  The field read, the page's script, tesseract
and the model are callbacks; every extraction has a timeout; a newer
question or a barge-in bumps a generation counter and late results are
dropped.  The screen capture and the PNG write before tesseract starts
are synchronous, exactly as in the @code{gowl_screen_text} tool.  The
one wait is typing a question in the minibuffer, in the door by hand.

@node Deixis Ask Customization
@section Customization

@table @code
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-voice
Answer spoken questions here (@code{t}).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-catch-all
Take unlisted questions (@code{t}).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-question-words
First words that make a catch-all question.
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-lead-ins
Words skipped at the start.
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-catch-all-routes
Routes the catch-all may take from (@code{(chat agent)}).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-whole-words
After a demonstrative: ask about the whole thing.
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-max-chars
Most characters of one thing sent (6000).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-context-lines
Lines either side of a lone line (5).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-error-lines
Most lines of one compilation error (15).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-ocr-box
The OCR box in layout pixels (@code{(900 . 420)}).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-ocr-language
Tesseract languages; @code{nil} is the screenshot module's.
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-web-min-chars
How much text the element under the pointer should have (200).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-deny-applies-to-ocr
The edit-field deny list also stops OCR (@code{t}).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-send-window-identity
Send a window's app id and title (@code{nil}).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-images
Also send another window's OCR box as a picture when the model takes
images (@code{t}).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-extract-timeout
Seconds to read one thing (45).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-followup-seconds
How long ``and that one?'' continues an answer (300).
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-provider
@itemx cmacs-deixis-ask-model
Override the text operations' provider and model.
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-system-prompt
@itemx cmacs-deixis-ask-voice-system-suffix
The system prompt, and what is added when the answer will be spoken.
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-menu
The questions the door by hand offers.
@item cmacs-deixis-ask-toast
Refusals and failures also as a bar toast (@code{t}).
@end table

@node Deixis Ask Internals
@section Additions, reaches and tests

Three additions to @file{cmacs-deixis.el}, all additive:
@code{cmacs-deixis-unclaimed-functions}, the hook
@code{cmacs-deixis-filter} runs after the commands;
@code{cmacs-deixis-referent-now} (and @code{cmacs-deixis--pointer-now}),
what the pointer is on at this moment with nothing spoken; and
@code{:frame-x} / @code{:frame-y} on a frame referent, the pixel it was
resolved from.

Reaches into other files, stated so that a change there knows about
here.  The voice path sets the loop's
@code{cmacs-voice-loop--stream-open} and @code{--tts-tail} and calls
@code{--set-state}, @code{--feed-reply} and
@code{cmacs-voice-loop-speak}.  Speech is tapped with @code{:before}
advice on @code{cmacs-ai-output-append}, @code{cmacs-ai-output-finish}
and @code{cmacs-ai-output--settle-followup}, installed at the first
spoken question --- the device the loop itself uses on
@code{cmacs-ai-chat--stream-callback} --- acting only on this file's
result buffer while a spoken question is in flight.  The follow-up path
reads @code{cmacs-ai-output--compose-marker}, @code{--session},
@code{--request} and @code{--done}.  The door by hand calls
@code{cmacs-gowl-ai-field--choose} and @code{--read-instruction}; the
OCR path calls @code{cmacs-gowl-tools--screen-text}; the deny check
calls @code{cmacs-gowl-edit-field--denied-p}.

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-deixis-ask-tests.el} is pure ERT, 37 tests.  The
compositor, gsurf, the edit-field session, tesseract, the model and the
voice loop are stubs; the redaction functions are stubbed too, so the
result does not depend on the privacy rules of the machine running the
suite.  The buffer extractor, the question table, the prompt,
@code{cmacs-ai-textops-stream} and the result window's follow-up path
are the real code.  Covered: extractor selection per referent kind and
the open registry; the thing chosen at a position and its size limits;
diagnostics; refusal of an excluded buffer, window, URL, denied window,
closed window, and of everything when the rules signal, each before any
read; redaction of foreign text; the OCR box arithmetic; field selection
falling through to OCR; the web view; the question table, including
what is @emph{not} taken; two referents end to end; a spoken answer
shown and spoken; ``and that one?'' reusing the session; barge-in; the
door by hand, the menu card and the AI menu entry.

Not covered, and needing a live session: the text-input read against a
real application, tesseract on real pixels, a page's JavaScript in
WebKit, a real provider's stream (in particular what a cancelled stream
delivers), and speech with a real barge-in.
